      "math_academy_id": "q-332796",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-2,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)+3{t}^{2}+1\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-2\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+L\\{1\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-2L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the results\n\n$$\nL\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,L\\{{t}^{2}\\}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\n$$\n\n we can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1={X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{6}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)-\\frac{2}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
